🔍 What is DISTINCT in SQL?
The DISTINCT keyword in SQL is used to remove duplicate rows from the result set and return only unique values.

✅ Syntax
sql
Copy
Edit
SELECT DISTINCT column1, column2, ...
FROM table_name;
🧱 Example Table: users
| id | name  | city      |
| -- | ----- | --------- |
| 1  | Afham | Lahore    |
| 2  | Ali   | Karachi   |
| 3  | Afham | Lahore    |
| 4  | Sara  | Islamabad |


🔹 Example 1: Distinct One Column
sql
Copy
Edit
SELECT DISTINCT city FROM users;

Result:

city
Lahore
Karachi
Islamabad

➡️ Duplicates are removed — "Lahore" appears only once.

🔹 Example 2: Distinct Multiple Columns
sql
Copy
Edit
SELECT DISTINCT name, city FROM users;
Result:

| name  | city      |
| ----- | --------- |
| Afham | Lahore    |
| Ali   | Karachi   |
| Sara  | Islamabad |


➡️ Removes duplicates based on the combination of name + city.

⚠️ Things to Note
| Feature                | Behavior                                    |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Case-sensitive?        | Depends on collation (usually not in MySQL) |
| Affects performance?   | Yes — may slow down queries on large data   |
| Works with `ORDER BY`? | ✅ Yes                                       |

🧠 Real-World Uses
Get a list of unique cities, user roles, product categories, etc.

Prevent duplicate data in reporting

Combine with COUNT to count unique values:

sql
Copy
Edit
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT city) FROM users;

✅ Summary
| Keyword    | Purpose                    |
| ---------- | -------------------------- |
| `DISTINCT` | Removes duplicate rows     |
| Used With  | `SELECT` statement         |
| Works On   | Single or multiple columns |


